#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163088} :activity/id #uuid "b95ad662-48db-5750-bd33-a69aa466aae5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050368 :topic/id #uuid "43c8acd9-2549-595b-b25f-fa9322986113" :topic/math-academy-id 3061} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163335 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $X$ is a discrete random variable with support $S_X$, and $Y = r(X)$ for some function $r$, then we can compute the expected value of $Y$ using the rule of the lazy statistician, which states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[Y] = \\sum_{x \\in S_X}r(x) \\, f_X(x)\n$$\n\nNow suppose that $W = r(X,Y)$ is a function of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively. Analogously to the case above, the **rule of the lazy statistician for two random variables** states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[W] =\\textrm{E}\\big[r(X,Y)\\big]=\\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}r(x,y) \\, f(x,y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y$.\n\nThe rule of the lazy statistician for two random variables is a helpful rule for two reasons:\n\n- Firstly, it allows us to compute $\\textrm{E}[W]$ without needing to find the PMF of $W$. This turns out to be very useful indeed.\n- Secondly, we can use this rule to prove many useful properties of random variables. For example, it can be used to prove that\n$\\textrm E[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm E[X] \\pm \\textrm E[Y]$.\nWe'll prove some useful results using our rule at the end of this lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "89c160d5-fa5c-59ab-980f-825724573c47" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6310 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6a244bac-441f-5193-b1f8-98989c0cf7bf" :step/math-academy-id 38191 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163089}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162117 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161683 :question/id #uuid "07dfee3a-03ad-5f25-937b-6025519c7d61" :question/math-academy-id "e-10123"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "600fd48d-930b-5f4e-b800-8992ab381285" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051001}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162403}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Rule to Discrete Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "63f9370a-f66d-53f4-b648-08490441016e" :step/math-academy-id 38193 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162118 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161684 :question/id #uuid "ea625b8d-d38d-5e55-9830-a56d7eb8204f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10124"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a64476b6-48ab-5533-8274-364a5decb3c2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049728}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162405}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Rule to Continuous Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "a5ea8edc-d9cc-5013-a915-c6bf7a0bde5e" :step/math-academy-id 38194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163095}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163336 :tutorial/content "Let's prove that for continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X+Y] = \\textrm{E}[X] + \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nr(x, y) = x+y\n$$\n\nBy the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X + Y] &= E[r(X, Y)] \\\\\n&= ∬_(R^{2})(x + y)f(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= ∬_(R^{2})xf(x, y)dxdy + ∬_(R^{2})yf(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}\\int_{- ∞}^{∞}xf(x, y)dxdy + \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}\\int_{- ∞}^{∞}yf(x, y)dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the integration domain is rectangular, we can swap the order of integration in the first integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X + Y] &= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}\\int_{- ∞}^{∞}xf(x, y)dydx + \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}\\int_{- ∞}^{∞}yf(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}x\\int_{- ∞}^{∞}f(x, y)dydx + \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}y\\int_{- ∞}^{∞}f(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}xf_{X}(x)dx + \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}yf_{Y}(y)dy \\\\\n&= E[X] + E[Y]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. Note that we made use of the fact that\n\n$$\nf_X(x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y, \\qquad f_Y(y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nThe proof for discrete random variables is similar." :tutorial/id #uuid "caf8d6da-5dbf-541c-a41d-7a805ab4ba2a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6312 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Linearity of Expectation"} :step/id #uuid "1352f892-d674-5a8e-aaf8-234c87647d82" :step/math-academy-id 38195 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163110}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163337 :tutorial/content "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:\\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician for two random variables states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big[r(X,Y)] = \\iint_{\\mathbb{R}^2} r(x,y) \\, f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}x \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nThe rule of the lazy statistician for two random variables is especially useful for continuous random variables since computing the PDF of $W = r(X, Y)$ can be challenging." :tutorial/id #uuid "bb0a375a-d3ae-56b3-bcbe-94b8cf287140" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6313 :tutorial/title "The Rule of the Lazy Statistician for Two Continuous Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "f1666e75-f57f-5368-b454-04acc16be94a" :step/math-academy-id 38196 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162120 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161686 :question/id #uuid "8e92b69d-1734-516d-8426-a44e03e907a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-10127"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e51358-060a-5117-bd7d-8909e695af48" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162404}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Rule to Continuous Random Variables: Non-Rectangular Domains"} :step/id #uuid "d1324a92-4dba-5324-9260-256e6b0f82e7" :step/math-academy-id 38211 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163110 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163342 :tutorial/content "Let's prove that for continuous *independent* random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[XY] = \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nr(x, y) = xy\n$$\n\nBy the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= E[r(X, Y)] \\\\\n&= ∬_(R^{2})xyf(x, y)dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X$ and $Y$ are independent by assumption, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y(y)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= ∬_(R^{2})xyf(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= ∬_(R^{2})xyf_{X}(x)f_{Y}(y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}\\int_{- ∞}^{∞}xyf_{X}(x)f_{Y}(y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}xf_{X}(x)dx \\cdot \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}yf_{Y}(y)dy \\\\\n&= E[X] \\cdot E[Y]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nThe proof for discrete random variables is similar." :tutorial/id #uuid "2fb7bc5c-c8f7-54a9-9d73-4fd2e4560220" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6447 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Product Rule for Independent Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "771cad2b-87eb-5646-ad5d-34d758789a03" :step/math-academy-id 38964}] :activity/title "The Rule of the Lazy Statistician for Two Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162195}]}
